### Hardware Reality: Spatial Locality & The Cache Line

Modern CPUs do not fetch single bytes from RAM. RAM is hundreds of times slower than the CPU core.

Instead, the CPU memory controller fetches memory in contiguous 64-byte chunks called a **Cache Line** into ultra-fast L1/L2/L3 hardware caches.

**Spatial Locality in ArrayList:**
* **`ArrayList`** stores references in a flat, contiguous memory array.
* When the CPU loads `elementData[0]`, the hardware pre-fetcher automatically pulls `elementData[1]` through `elementData[7]` into the L1 cache simultaneously.
* This principle of **Spatial Locality** makes scanning an `ArrayList` exceptionally fast.

---

### Pointer Chasing: The Death of LinkedList Throughput

In **`LinkedList`**, each node is allocated independently on the Heap at random, fragmented memory addresses.

**The Cost of Pointer Chasing:**
* Accessing `node.next` requires the CPU to jump to a completely different memory address, resulting in a **CPU Cache Miss**.
* The CPU must stall for hundreds of clock cycles while waiting for data to arrive from main RAM. This latency penalty is known as **Pointer Chasing**.

---

### Performance Traversal in Code

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.List;

public class CacheLocalityDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int count = 100_000;
        List<Integer> arrayList = new ArrayList<>(count);
        List<Integer> linkedList = new LinkedList<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
            arrayList.add(i);
            linkedList.add(i);
        }

        // Iteration test: Sequential traversal
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        long sumA = 0;
        for (int num : arrayList) sumA += num;
        long durationA = System.nanoTime() - start;

        start = System.nanoTime();
        long sumL = 0;
        for (int num : linkedList) sumL += num;
        long durationL = System.nanoTime() - start;

        System.out.printf("ArrayList Traversal:  %8d ns (Fast due to Spatial Locality)\n", durationA);
        System.out.printf("LinkedList Traversal: %8d ns (Slow due to Pointer Chasing)\n", durationL);
    }
}
```

**Console Output:**
```text
ArrayList Traversal:   1524300 ns (Fast due to Spatial Locality)
LinkedList Traversal:  7892100 ns (Slow due to Pointer Chasing)
```

---

### Industry Guideline: The 99% Rule

Always default to `ArrayList`. Use `ArrayDeque` when you need a queue or stack. Reserve `LinkedList` only when splicing pre-positioned nodes without index lookups.

