practiceProblems.problemStatement:
A developer wrote a user search service using `LinkedList<String>`:
```java
public static List<String> getPage(LinkedList<String> users, int page, int pageSize) {
    List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
    int start = page * pageSize;
    for (int i = start; i < start + pageSize && i < users.size(); i++) {
        result.add(users.get(i)); // ACCIDENTAL O(N) IN EACH ITERATION
    }
    return result;
}
```
Because `users.get(i)` is called on a `LinkedList`, page lookups for deep pages (e.g. page 100) become extremely slow!

Your Task:
1. Refactor `getPage` to accept `List<String> users`.
2. Use `users.subList(start, end)` to extract the page in $O(1)$ constant time!
3. In `Main.main()`, test with an `ArrayList<String>` of 20 users `["User0", "User1", ... "User19"]`.
4. Retrieve `page = 1, pageSize = 5` (users 5 through 9) and print:
   `Page 1 Results: [User5, User6, User7, User8, User9]`

practiceProblems.solutionCode:
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {
    public static List<String> getPage(List<String> users, int page, int pageSize) {
        int start = page * pageSize;
        int end = Math.min(start + pageSize, users.size());
        if (start >= users.size()) return new ArrayList<>();
        return new ArrayList<>(users.subList(start, end));
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> users = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < 20; i++) {
            users.add("User" + i);
        }

        List<String> page1 = getPage(users, 1, 5);
        System.out.println("Page 1 Results: " + page1);
    }
}

